{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"On node1, enforce a 100 MB filesystem-level storage limit for datauser covering /srv/data.","status":"satisfied","summary":"datauser exists with /srv/data as its home and owned directory. XFS user-quota accounting and enforcement are active, with 100M soft and hard limits. A 101 MiB write in /srv/data failed with “Disk quota exceeded”; after a verified restart, the same quota, data, and enforcement behavior persisted. The quota applies to all datauser-owned files on the root filesystem, which is broader than only /srv/data but still enforces the requested ceiling there.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7f11a9307a654b04","cmd-89c38874f3304e76","cmd-f62636a8a24c4965","cmd-b6f5b03ee82b4227","cmd-0e8fccc841c8439f","cmd-a1e0d59eebfa401a","cmd-c54ae5cd83eb4f54","cmd-ce53a6b6ed3b4bc9","cmd-0ec2ae0db6904e6c"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.93,"summary":"Final hygiene was strong: quota test files were removed, no failed services or new listeners appeared, and package health remained intact. Two unnecessary mutations occurred in cmd-276a7edf2a764796: it temporarily wrote malformed literal text into /etc/fstab, later corrected by cmd-359424b37a75451c, and created /etc/fstab.pre-datauser-quota without any captured removal, leaving an apparent backup-file residue. The malformed edit was brief and fully repaired; the retained backup is low risk. Other mutations had clear roles in configuration, activation, testing, restart evidence, or cleanup.","evidence_ids":["cmd-276a7edf2a764796","cmd-359424b37a75451c","cmd-0ec2ae0db6904e6c","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The requested filesystem-enforced 100 MB limit is directly demonstrated and survived restart. The provider restart protocol is complete: healthy pre-restart quota/data state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, recovered state, preserved data, and repeated enforcement were captured; the final boot identity also matches the lifecycle observation. Only minor hygiene defects reduce the score.","limitations":["The XFS user quota is filesystem-wide for datauser rather than pathname-scoped exclusively to /srv/data, so it may also restrict datauser-owned files elsewhere on the root filesystem.","The final evidence did not inventory /etc/fstab.pre-datauser-quota; its apparent retention is inferred from its recorded creation and the absence of a removal command in the complete timeline."]}
